'''Tochigi Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|栃木県}}, ''Tochigi-ken'') is a [[landlocked]] [[prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located in the interior highlands of the island of [[Ishijima]], bordered by [[Gunma Prefecture|Gunma]] to the north, [[Ishijima Prefecture|Ishijima]] to the northeast, [[Noto Prefecture|Noto]] to the east, [[Ehime Prefecture|Ehime]] to the south, [[Haruno Prefecture|Haruno]] to the southwest, and [[Shiga Prefecture|Shiga]] to the west. Its population as of 2026 was 1,785,024, with approximately 39% concentrated in the prefectural capital of [[Tochigi, Tochigi|Tochigi]] (pop. 753,068). Tochigi is the only fully landlocked prefecture on Ishijima, a geographic reality that has shaped its economy, culture, and historical development in ways distinct from its coastal neighbours.

The prefecture is defined by its mountainous terrain. The [[Asuka Range]] ({{lang|ja|飛鳥山脈}}, ''Asuka-sanmyaku'') dominates the northern border, with [[Mount Asuka]] ({{lang|ja|飛鳥山}}, 1,892 m) serving as the highest peak and the natural boundary between Tochigi, Gunma, and Ishijima prefectures. The interior consists of a series of elevated basins and valleys—the largest being the [[Tochigi Basin]]—that support intensive agriculture and the prefecture's traditional craft industries. The absence of a coastline has historically isolated Tochigi from the maritime trade networks that enriched its neighbours, but it has also preserved a distinctive inland culture centred on temple architecture, lacquerware, hot springs, and highland agriculture.

== History ==

=== Ancient and pre-shogunate period ===
The Tochigi interior was originally inhabited by [[Atsu]] highland communities who practised slash-and-burn agriculture and harvested mountain vegetables and game from the dense forests of the Asuka Range. The [[Kajika]] maritime expansion largely bypassed the landlocked interior, and the Atsu communities of the Tochigi highlands developed a distinctive culture adapted to the mountainous environment, including the cultivation of persimmons, tea, and buckwheat on terraced hillside farms.

The region's strategic importance emerged during the [[Sengoku period (Okaiken)|Sengoku period]], when the mountain passes of the Asuka Range became critical corridors for armies moving between the Ishijima coast and the interior. The [[Battle of Asuka Pass]] (1482) was one of the defining engagements of the period, in which the [[Honda clan]]—then still minor ''daimyō''—secured control of the mountain routes that would later prove essential to their rise to the shogunate.

=== The Honda Shogunate and the Tochigi Basin ===
Under the [[Honda Shogunate]], the Tochigi Basin became a key agricultural and craft production centre for the shogunal economy. The [[Tochigi Domain]] ({{lang|ja|栃木藩}}, ''Tochigi-han'') was established as a direct Honda retainer territory, governed by the [[Kamigori clan]]—hereditary vassals of the Honda who maintained the mountain passes and supplied the shogunate with timber, lacquerware, and the prized Tochigi persimmons that were served at the shogunal court.

The Shogunate period saw the development of the [[Tochigi Lacquerware School]] ({{lang|ja|栃木漆器流}}, ''Tochigi shikki-ryū''), a distinctive style that used the region's abundant ''urushi'' trees and the cold mountain fog of the Asuka highlands to produce a lacquer finish characterised by its deep amber hue—a contrast to the darker black-and-red lacquers of the Noto and coastal schools. The [[Tochigi Temple Route]], a network of Shingon Buddhist temples built along the mountain passes, became a major pilgrimage destination during the shogunate period, and the surviving temples of the route are among the finest examples of pre-Restoration religious architecture in Okaiken.

=== Modern era ===
The [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871 converted the Tochigi Domain into Tochigi Prefecture. The landlocked geography initially posed challenges for the modernising economy—the prefecture lacked the deep-water ports that drove industrialisation in neighbouring [[Aizawa Prefecture|Aizawa]] and [[Ishijima Prefecture|Ishijima]], and the mountainous terrain made railway construction expensive and slow. The [[Tochigi Line]], connecting the prefectural capital to the Ishijima Shinkansen network, was not completed until 1924, decades after the coastal rail lines.

The post-[[Great Adonian War]] period saw Tochigi Prefecture find its economic niche in precision manufacturing and highland agriculture. The [[Tochigi Precision Works]], established in 1958, became a leading manufacturer of optical instruments and measurement equipment, leveraging the region's traditional lacquerware craftsmanship to produce precision-coated optical surfaces. The prefecture's hot springs—the [[Asuka Onsen]] cluster in the northern highlands—became a major domestic tourism draw, and the [[Regional Heritage Tourism Act]] of 2002 provided additional funding to develop the Tochigi Temple Route as a cultural tourism destination.

== Geography ==

Tochigi Prefecture is the only fully landlocked prefecture on the island of Ishijima, occupying the interior highlands between the coastal prefectures that surround it on all sides. The prefecture is bordered by [[Gunma Prefecture|Gunma]] to the north, [[Ishijima Prefecture|Ishijima]] to the northeast, [[Noto Prefecture|Noto]] to the east, [[Ehime Prefecture|Ehime]] to the south, [[Haruno Prefecture|Haruno]] to the southwest, and [[Shiga Prefecture|Shiga]] to the west.

The [[Asuka Range]] dominates the northern and eastern borders, with [[Mount Asuka]] (1,892 m) serving as the highest peak and the natural boundary between Tochigi, Gunma, and Ishijima. The range creates a dramatic landscape of steep forested slopes, deep river gorges, and high mountain passes that have historically controlled movement between the coast and the interior. The [[Asuka Pass]] ({{lang|ja|飛鳥峠}}, ''Asuka-tōge''), at 1,246 m, is the most important of these routes and has been a strategic military and trade corridor since the Sengoku period.

The interior consists of the [[Tochigi Basin]]—a broad, elevated valley at approximately 400–600 m elevation that supports the prefecture's agricultural heartland—and a series of smaller intermontane valleys that extend southward toward the Ehime border. The [[Inagawa River]] ({{lang|ja|猪名川}}, ''Inagawa''), the prefecture's longest waterway at 78 km, flows from the Asuka highlands through the Tochigi Basin and provides irrigation for the terraced farms of the interior.

