== Economy ==

Tochigi Prefecture's economy is defined by its landlocked, mountainous geography. Unlike the coastal prefectures of Ishijima, which built their wealth on maritime trade and heavy industry, Tochigi has developed a distinctive economic profile centred on highland agriculture, precision manufacturing, traditional crafts, and domestic tourism. The prefecture's GDP per capita is slightly below the Ishijima average, reflecting the absence of a port and the higher transportation costs of its interior location, but its specialisation in high-value agricultural and craft products has created a stable and resilient economic base.

=== Highland agriculture ===
The Tochigi Basin and the terraced slopes of the Asuka foothills support intensive tropical highland agriculture. The elevated terrain creates a slightly cooler microclimate than the coastal lowlands, allowing the cultivation of crops that would struggle in the hotter, more humid conditions of the coast. The signature products are:

Highland horticulture is defined by three signature staple crops adapted to the basin's sharp day-night temperature swings. The sweet, firm-fleshed Tochigi persimmon ({{lang|ja|栃木柿}}, ''Tochigi-gaki'') is cellar-cured and sun-dried in the crisp winter air to yield premium hoshigaki, celebrated annually during the [[Tochigi Persimmon Festival]]. In the mist-shrouded Inagawa River valley, artisanal growers cultivate Tochigi tea ({{lang|ja|栃木茶}}, ''Tochigi-cha''), producing an aromatic green tea noted for its deep, smoky flavor. Along the higher elevations of the Asuka foothills, farmers cultivate cold-hardy buckwheat, making the prefecture one of the largest producers of milling grain for the domestic noodle industry and anchoring the popular culinary route known as the [[Tochigi Soba Road]].

=== Precision manufacturing ===
The [[Tochigi Precision Works]], established in 1958 in the suburbs of [[Tochigi, Tochigi|Tochigi]], is the prefecture's largest industrial employer and a nationally significant manufacturer of optical instruments, measurement equipment, and precision-coated surfaces. The company's origins lie in the traditional lacquerware craftsmanship of the Tochigi School, which was adapted to industrial applications in the post-war period—much as the Noto lacquerware tradition gave rise to the contact lens industry.

The precision manufacturing cluster, centred on the [[Kamigori Industrial Park]], employs approximately 8,200 workers and generates annual revenues of 円62 billion. The cluster's products include optical lenses for semiconductor manufacturing equipment, precision measurement instruments for the automotive and aerospace industries, and specialised coatings for medical devices.

=== Traditional crafts ===
The [[Tochigi Lacquerware School]] ({{lang|ja|栃木漆器流}}, ''Tochigi shikki-ryū'') is one of the three great lacquerware traditions of Ishijima, alongside the Noto and Kiso schools. The Tochigi style is distinguished by its amber-toned finish, produced by the region's unique ''urushi'' varietals and the cold, fog-curing process of the highland workshops. The [[Tochigi Lacquerware Cooperative]], based in [[Ichikawa, Tochigi|Ichikawa]], maintains approximately 40 active workshops and produces both traditional ceremonial pieces and modern design objects.

The prefecture is also known for its [[Zelkova]] woodwork ({{lang|ja|栃木欅細工}}, ''Tochigi-keyaki-zaiku''), using the abundant zelkova forests of the Asuka foothills to produce high-quality furniture, temple fittings, and architectural elements. The [[Tochigi Temple Route]] restoration programme, funded by the Regional Heritage Tourism Act, has provided a steady market for the traditional woodworkers who supply the temple restoration projects.

=== Onsen tourism ===
The [[Asuka Onsen]] cluster, a group of 12 hot spring resorts in the northern highlands near Mount Asuka, is the prefecture's most important tourism asset. The volcanic geology of the Asuka Range produces a range of mineral-rich thermal waters, and the resorts combine traditional ''ryokan'' hospitality with modern wellness facilities. The [[Asuka Onsen]] is particularly noted for its iron-rich "red water" springs ({{lang|ja|赤泉}}, ''Akasen''), which are believed to have therapeutic properties for circulatory conditions.

The [[Tochigi Temple Route]], a 68-kilometre pilgrimage trail connecting 23 Shingon Buddhist temples along the mountain passes, draws approximately 320,000 visitors annually. The trail combines cultural heritage with mountain hiking, and the [[Shiso, Tochigi|Shiso]] Temple—perched on a cliff overlooking the Inagawa gorge—is considered one of the most photographed religious sites in Ishijima.
Tochigi's industrial sector combines specialized volcanic pyrometallurgy with heavy transit testing. Local foundries utilize traditional Kazan-tetsu techniques to forge heavy seismic structural dampers, manganese-alloy rail frogs, and wear-resistant cutting heads for deep-rock tunnel-boring machines. In the mountainous northern highlands, automotive and rail consortiums maintain high-altitude vehicle proving grounds, subjecting next-generation fuel-cell lorries and pneumatic freight capsules to severe sub-zero cold-weather stress tests.

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Tochigi Prefecture's cuisine reflects its landlocked, highland geography. The signature dish is ''Tochigi soba'' ({{lang|ja|栃木蕎麦}}), made from the prefecture's highland buckwheat and served with a dipping broth flavoured with the region's distinctive smoky tea. The [[Tochigi Soba Road]] in the northern highlands connects over 30 soba restaurants, each with its own recipe and style.

Other local specialties include ''hoshigaki'' (dried persimmon, served as a dessert or snack), ''yuba'' (tofu skin, a specialty of the temple cuisine tradition), and ''Asuka nabe'' (a hot pot made with local mountain vegetables, buckwheat noodles, and chicken, served at the onsen resorts during the cooler dry season).

=== The Temple Route ===
The [[Tochigi Temple Route]] is the prefecture's most significant cultural institution. The 23 temples of the route, built between the 9th and 16th centuries, represent the finest surviving examples of Shingon Buddhist architecture in Ishijima. The route was originally a pilgrimage path for mountain ascetics, and the temples were positioned at spiritually significant points along the mountain passes—waterfalls, cliff faces, and ancient ''sugi'' (cedar) groves.

The [[Shiso Temple]] ({{lang|ja|宍粟寺}}, ''Shiso-ji''), built in 1284 on a cliff overlooking the Inagawa gorge, is the most famous of the route's temples. Its main hall, supported by cantilevered zelkova beams that project over the gorge, is a masterpiece of medieval engineering and is designated a [[National Treasure (Okaiken)|National Treasure]].

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Tochigi Line]] connects [[Tochigi Station]] to the [[Ishijima Shinkansen]] network at [[Aizawa Station]] via the [[Asuka Tunnel]], a 14.2 km bore through the Asuka Range that was completed in 1972. The line provides high-speed rail access to [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]]. The [[Inagawa Local Line]], a conventional rail service, runs through the Tochigi Basin connecting the interior towns.

=== Road ===
The [[Tochigi Expressway]] is the primary road link, connecting the prefectural capital to the [[Ishijima Coastal Expressway]] at the Gunma border. The [[Asuka Pass Road]], a scenic mountain highway that crosses the Asuka Range at 1,246 m, is one of the most celebrated driving routes in Okaiken but is frequently closed during the wet season due to landslides.

== Education ==

The [[Tochigi Institute of Optical Sciences]], affiliated with the Tochigi Precision Works, is the prefecture's premier research institution, specialising in precision optics, coating technology, and measurement science. The [[Asuka Mountain Academy]], a private institution in the northern highlands, is known for its programmes in traditional crafts and temple architecture.

== Notable people ==

